344-40.value:
This is a controlled computational simulation study using a two-dimensional Gray–Scott reaction–diffusion model. The study evaluates five preregistered conditions using the same frozen numerical implementation and ten explicit confirmatory seeds.

The five conditions are:

sustained driving in a preregistered pattern-forming parameter regime;
sustained driving in a preregistered non-pattern-forming comparator regime;
removal of feed/driving at PDE step 3500 after pattern formation;
standardised local damage to the central region at PDE step 3500 while sustained driving continues;
change from periodic to no-flux boundary conditions at PDE step 3500 after pattern formation.

The primary design is paired across registered seeds wherever conditions share the same initialisation procedure, allowing the effect of the manipulation to be compared while controlling for seed-specific initial variation. Conditions are specified in advance rather than randomly assigned.

The sustained-pattern condition tests whether the frozen pattern-forming regime produces reproducible non-trivial spatial heterogeneity. The non-pattern comparator tests whether the registered control regime approaches effective spatial homogeneity. The drive-removal intervention tests whether established pattern structure depends causally upon continued external driving. The local-damage condition tests selective disruption and recovery under continued driving. The boundary condition manipulation tests robustness of the established pattern to a registered change in system boundary.

Measurements include V-field spatial heterogeneity and preregistered descriptive spatial-pattern measures. Normalised mutual-information and spectral descriptors are subject to a preregistered amplitude gate to prevent near-zero fields from being misclassified as structured. Local-damage recovery is additionally measured within the perturbed central region.

All ten confirmatory seeds are held out from development. Development-pilot data from three separate seeds were used only to validate the implementation and finalise the frozen version 1.1.0 protocol. Confirmatory raw fields, trajectory logs, provenance, analyses, figures, tables and decision records will be generated automatically under the frozen manifests.

The study is designed to test driven pattern persistence only. A favourable result will not be interpreted as evidence of self-maintenance, organisational closure, life or any higher-level Theory of Everything dependency.

344-47.value:
This study generates new computational simulation data from a frozen two-dimensional Gray–Scott reaction–diffusion model. No human, animal or pre-existing observational dataset is sampled.

The sampling frame consists of ten explicit preregistered confirmatory random seeds applied to five frozen experimental conditions:

sustained driving in the preregistered pattern-forming regime;
sustained driving in the preregistered non-pattern-forming comparator regime;
removal of driving at PDE step 3500 after pattern formation;
standardised local damage at PDE step 3500 while sustained driving continues;
replacement of periodic with no-flux boundary conditions at PDE step 3500 after pattern formation.

The same registered confirmatory seed set is used across conditions where the initialisation procedure is shared, permitting paired condition comparisons while controlling for seed-dependent variation in the initial state.

All confirmatory simulations will be generated only after this preregistration becomes public and the software execution gate is changed from FROZEN_PREREGISTRATION_READY_UNRUN to CLEARED_FOR_CONFIRMATORY_EXECUTION. The ten confirmatory seeds are disjoint from the three seeds used during development and pilot validation.

Each valid simulation run will archive the raw (u) and (v) fields or the registered field snapshots required by the frozen protocol, trajectory-level measurements, intervention labels, seed identity, configuration and manifest hashes, software provenance and derived preregistered measurements.

A run will be excluded from scientific analysis only if a preregistered invalid-run condition is met, such as manifest or configuration mismatch, environment validation failure, seed mismatch, corrupted output or required provenance failure. Failure of a scientific hypothesis is not an exclusion criterion and remains part of the confirmatory evidence.

No confirmatory seed will be replaced because its outcome is unusual, unfavourable or inconsistent with the preregistered prediction. Any rerun required because of a technically invalid execution will retain a documented audit trail identifying the invalid run and the reason for rerunning it.

344-51.value:
The confirmatory sample contains ten preregistered independent simulation seeds.

Each of the five experimental conditions will be executed using all ten confirmatory seeds, giving:

50\ \text{planned condition-level simulation runs}.
]

The primary independent sampling unit is the simulation seed within a condition. Where the same seed is used across related conditions, those observations form a paired comparison rather than independent samples.

Measurements recorded at multiple PDE time steps, spatial lattice sites or field snapshots within one simulation run are repeated observations nested within that run and are not treated as additional independent replicates.

The confirmatory design therefore contains:

10 independent seed identities;
10 runs per condition;
5 preregistered conditions;
50 planned condition-level runs in total.

The three development-pilot seeds are not included in the confirmatory sample size and will not contribute to confirmatory estimates, uncertainty calculations or decision rules.

344-75.value:
No centering, standardisation, logarithmic transformation or outcome-dependent transformation of the primary (U)- or (V)-field measurements is planned.

Experimental condition is represented by fixed categorical identifiers corresponding to the five preregistered conditions:

sustained pattern regime;
sustained non-pattern comparator;
drive removal;
local damage and recovery;
boundary modification.

Seed identity is retained as a fixed identifier and is used to align paired descriptive comparisons across conditions. It is not recoded according to outcome.

Several preregistered derived variables are calculated directly from the raw fields:

whole-field (V)-field standard deviation;
central-region mean (V);
immediate retained fraction after local damage;
immediate proportional deficit;
time to heterogeneity collapse after drive removal;
time to 90-percent local recovery;
terminal recovery fraction;
active spatial fraction;
adjacent-cell spatial mutual information;
spectral peak fraction.

For the local-damage analysis, proportional quantities are formed relative to the last registered pre-damage central-region mean (V).

For spatial mutual information and spectral peak fraction, a preregistered amplitude gate is applied: when whole-field (V)-field standard deviation is below (10^{-5}), these two descriptive pattern measures are assigned a value of zero. This prevents numerical structure in an effectively homogeneous field from being treated as substantive spatial organisation.

No transformation will be selected or modified in response to confirmatory outcomes.

344-79.value:
All valid confirmatory runs from all ten preregistered seeds and all five preregistered conditions will be included in the confirmatory analysis.

No observation will be excluded because it is an outlier, produces an unexpected pattern, contradicts the preregistered prediction or materially changes the aggregate result. No statistical outlier-removal rule will be applied to scientifically valid runs.

A simulation run is excluded from scientific analysis only if it satisfies a preregistered technical invalidity condition. These include:

manifest validation failure;
mismatch between the executed manifest and its frozen hash;
mismatch between a referenced configuration and its frozen hash;
execution with an unregistered or incorrect seed;
Gray–Scott environment validation failure;
non-finite numerical values or numerical failure;
corrupted or incomplete required raw output;
failure of required provenance or integrity verification.

A technically invalid run is recorded as INVALID_RUN and remains preserved in the audit trail with the reason for invalidity. It may be rerun using the same preregistered seed after the technical problem has been corrected.

Scientific failure is not an exclusion criterion. A valid run in which a hypothesis fails remains part of the evidence and is classified accordingly. Thus:

[
\texttt{VALID_NEGATIVE} \neq \texttt{INVALID_RUN}.
]

The three development-pilot seeds are excluded from all confirmatory analyses because they were used during protocol development. They remain archived separately as development evidence.

